1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,133
Who Gets Rich When You Buy GTA 6?

2
00:00:03,213 --> 00:00:05,293
November 19, 2026.

3
00:00:05,293 --> 00:00:10,077
After thirteen years and two delays,
Grand Theft Auto Six is finally out.

4
00:00:10,397 --> 00:00:13,997
The standard edition costs seventy-nine
dollars and ninety-nine cents.

5
00:00:13,997 --> 00:00:17,241
Ten dollars more than the usual price
of a big new console game.

6
00:00:17,241 --> 00:00:20,921
The last one in the series, Grand Theft
Auto Five, set a record when it

7
00:00:20,921 --> 00:00:22,281
launched in 2013.

8
00:00:22,281 --> 00:00:25,288
More than eight hundred million
dollars on its first day.

9
00:00:25,768 --> 00:00:29,768
So this time, millions of people are
handing over eighty dollars each.

10
00:00:29,768 --> 00:00:32,248
But who actually ends up with that money?

11
00:00:32,248 --> 00:00:33,928
The company that made the game?

12
00:00:33,928 --> 00:00:35,528
The console in your living room?

13
00:00:35,528 --> 00:00:36,528
The store?

14
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:38,400
Or someone you'd never expect?

15
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:40,560
This is Billions and Bulls.

16
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:42,400
Let's follow the money.

17
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:45,599
Part 1 Thirteen Years and Eighty Dollars

18
00:00:45,599 --> 00:00:49,599
To understand the money in this
game, start with the last one.

19
00:00:49,599 --> 00:00:52,374
Grand Theft Auto Five came out in 2013.

20
00:00:52,374 --> 00:00:56,214
According to its publisher, Take-Two
Interactive, it has now sold close

21
00:00:56,214 --> 00:00:58,682
to two hundred thirty million copies.

22
00:00:58,682 --> 00:01:03,082
That's copies shipped to stores and sold
digitally, across three generations

23
00:01:03,082 --> 00:01:04,602
of consoles and PCs.

24
00:01:04,602 --> 00:01:07,699
For thirteen years, it never
really stopped selling.

25
00:01:08,099 --> 00:01:11,219
It also set records nobody had
seen in entertainment.

26
00:01:11,219 --> 00:01:14,739
Eight hundred fifteen point seven million
dollars in its first twenty-four

27
00:01:14,739 --> 00:01:15,739
hours.

28
00:01:15,379 --> 00:01:18,865
One billion dollars in three days,
according to Guinness World Records.

29
00:01:19,025 --> 00:01:24,145
No movie, no album and no game had ever
reached a billion that fast.

30
00:01:24,225 --> 00:01:26,906
So expectations for the
sequel were enormous.

31
00:01:26,986 --> 00:01:30,986
Take-Two said pre-orders were tracking, in
its words, far in excess of its

32
00:01:30,986 --> 00:01:32,266
own expectations.

33
00:01:32,266 --> 00:01:35,706
The company expects about eight billion
dollars in sales for its fiscal

34
00:01:35,706 --> 00:01:38,561
year, and most of that growth
comes from this one game.

35
00:01:38,721 --> 00:01:42,721
Analysts at Wells Fargo reportedly expect
around sixty-five million copies

36
00:01:42,721 --> 00:01:44,721
sold in that year alone.

37
00:01:44,961 --> 00:01:46,481
And then there's the price.

38
00:01:46,481 --> 00:01:50,349
For years, seventy dollars was the
ceiling for a big console game.

39
00:01:50,509 --> 00:01:52,669
Grand Theft Auto Six starts at eighty.

40
00:01:52,669 --> 00:01:55,149
A bigger ultimate edition
costs one hundred.

41
00:01:55,149 --> 00:01:58,669
Early reviews have been glowing, so
most fans don't seem to mind.

42
00:01:58,669 --> 00:02:00,557
It's also a test for the whole industry.

43
00:02:00,557 --> 00:02:04,156
If the biggest game in the world can charge
eighty dollars, other publishers

44
00:02:04,156 --> 00:02:05,437
will be watching closely.

45
00:02:05,437 --> 00:02:08,803
A price ceiling, once broken,
rarely goes back down.

46
00:02:09,282 --> 00:02:12,162
Now, let's take one of those
eighty-dollar copies and follow it.

47
00:02:12,162 --> 00:02:16,242
Because the moment you click buy,
that money starts splitting up.

48
00:02:17,138 --> 00:02:19,058
Part 2 The Console's Cut

49
00:02:19,058 --> 00:02:22,658
Let's say you buy the game digitally,
straight from the store on your PlayStation

50
00:02:22,658 --> 00:02:24,018
or Xbox.

51
00:02:24,578 --> 00:02:27,191
The first slice goes to the
company that made your console.

52
00:02:27,431 --> 00:02:29,031
Sony, or Microsoft.

53
00:02:29,031 --> 00:02:33,271
For years, the standard cut on console
stores has been about thirty percent.

54
00:02:33,271 --> 00:02:36,471
On an eighty-dollar game, that's
about twenty-four dollars.

55
00:02:36,471 --> 00:02:38,529
Before the publisher sees anything.

56
00:02:39,009 --> 00:02:40,209
Why so much?

57
00:02:40,209 --> 00:02:42,369
Because consoles work a bit like printers.

58
00:02:42,369 --> 00:02:46,289
The machine is often sold close to cost,
sometimes at a loss, to get it

59
00:02:46,289 --> 00:02:47,982
into as many homes as possible.

60
00:02:47,982 --> 00:02:51,742
The money is made later, on the games and
subscriptions people buy for it.

61
00:02:51,742 --> 00:02:54,702
Every game sold on the
console's store pays a toll.

62
00:02:54,702 --> 00:02:56,838
The cut also pays for the store itself.

63
00:02:56,838 --> 00:03:00,518
The servers that deliver a huge game to
millions of homes on the same day,

64
00:03:00,518 --> 00:03:03,718
the payment processing,
refunds and fraud checks.

65
00:03:03,878 --> 00:03:06,515
Microsoft has looked at
lowering that toll before.

66
00:03:06,515 --> 00:03:09,955
Internal documents showed it once
considered cutting its console share to

67
00:03:09,955 --> 00:03:10,955
twelve percent.

68
00:03:10,915 --> 00:03:14,103
A spokesperson later said that
change wouldn't happen on Xbox.

69
00:03:14,103 --> 00:03:18,103
And since Grand Theft Auto Six is only on
consoles at launch, with no PC

70
00:03:18,103 --> 00:03:22,567
version yet, every single digital copy goes
through one of those two stores.

71
00:03:22,887 --> 00:03:27,607
So for each digital copy, about twenty-four
dollars goes to Sony or Microsoft,

72
00:03:27,607 --> 00:03:30,115
and about fifty-six dollars
goes to Take-Two.

73
00:03:30,115 --> 00:03:34,355
If tens of millions of people buy
digitally, the console makers earn billions

74
00:03:34,355 --> 00:03:36,755
from a game they didn't make.

75
00:03:37,851 --> 00:03:40,091
Part 3 The Box on the Shelf

76
00:03:40,091 --> 00:03:44,011
Now let's say you buy a physical
copy instead, at a store.

77
00:03:44,011 --> 00:03:47,357
Here, the split is harder to see,
because nobody publishes it.

78
00:03:47,597 --> 00:03:51,357
But one consultant who advises investors in
the games industry, Serkan Toto,

79
00:03:51,357 --> 00:03:53,837
has laid out a typical example.

80
00:03:54,157 --> 00:03:56,317
The store keeps around thirty percent.

81
00:03:56,317 --> 00:03:58,670
It has rent, staff and shelves to pay for.

82
00:03:58,910 --> 00:04:02,350
Then the console maker still gets a cut,
even though the game never touched

83
00:04:02,350 --> 00:04:03,550
its online store.

84
00:04:03,550 --> 00:04:07,648
Around fifteen percent, as a licensing fee
for making a game for its machine.

85
00:04:07,888 --> 00:04:12,128
And about five percent goes to making
and shipping the box itself.

86
00:04:12,288 --> 00:04:15,968
Using those rough percentages on an
eighty-dollar game, the publisher keeps

87
00:04:15,968 --> 00:04:18,060
around forty dollars from a physical copy.

88
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:20,780
Compared with about fifty-six
from a digital one.

89
00:04:20,780 --> 00:04:24,300
That gap helps explain why game
companies love digital sales.

90
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:27,274
And why physical game shelves
keep getting smaller.

91
00:04:27,594 --> 00:04:31,914
And on top of the eighty dollars, there's
one more slice most people forget.

92
00:04:31,914 --> 00:04:34,799
Sales tax, or value-added tax in Europe.

93
00:04:35,039 --> 00:04:38,479
That one goes to the government, before
anyone in the games business sees

94
00:04:38,479 --> 00:04:39,919
a cent.

95
00:04:40,079 --> 00:04:44,079
If you're enjoying the story so far,
subscribing helps the channel a lot.

96
00:04:44,079 --> 00:04:48,160
Because next, we follow the money into
Take-Two, and then into a place you

97
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:49,200
wouldn't expect.

98
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:51,360
The British tax system.

99
00:04:52,609 --> 00:04:54,849
Part 4 Inside Take-Two

100
00:04:54,849 --> 00:04:58,769
So Take-Two ends up with somewhere between
forty and fifty-six dollars per

101
00:04:58,769 --> 00:04:59,769
copy.

102
00:04:59,329 --> 00:05:00,329
Is that profit?

103
00:05:00,289 --> 00:05:01,790
Not even close.

104
00:05:02,270 --> 00:05:05,070
First, that money has to pay
for the game itself.

105
00:05:05,070 --> 00:05:08,910
Rockstar Games, the studio inside
Take-Two that makes Grand Theft Auto,

106
00:05:08,910 --> 00:05:12,500
has spent years building it, with
studios in several countries.

107
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:16,180
Take-Two has never said how much
Grand Theft Auto Six cost.

108
00:05:16,180 --> 00:05:20,153
For comparison, Grand Theft Auto Five
reportedly cost around one hundred

109
00:05:20,153 --> 00:05:21,820
thirty-seven million dollars to develop.

110
00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:25,820
The new one is widely expected
to have cost far more.

111
00:05:25,980 --> 00:05:29,980
Then there's marketing, servers, customer
support, and the people who will

112
00:05:29,980 --> 00:05:32,243
keep updating the game for years.

113
00:05:32,563 --> 00:05:36,163
And here's a detail that shows how
much rides on this one game.

114
00:05:36,163 --> 00:05:39,683
In the quarter before launch,
Take-Two actually lost money.

115
00:05:39,683 --> 00:05:41,523
Fifty-four million dollars.

116
00:05:41,523 --> 00:05:43,926
Its basketball game sold
less than expected.

117
00:05:44,086 --> 00:05:47,286
Its shares still rose more than eight
percent the next morning, because

118
00:05:47,286 --> 00:05:49,046
investors were betting on one thing.

119
00:05:49,046 --> 00:05:51,206
Grand Theft Auto Six.

120
00:05:51,446 --> 00:05:55,025
So in a sense, the eighty dollars you
pay isn't just buying a game.

121
00:05:55,025 --> 00:05:59,505
It's paying back years of spending that
happened before you ever saw a trailer.

122
00:05:59,745 --> 00:06:03,100
And it's worth knowing where
Take-Two's money usually comes from.

123
00:06:03,260 --> 00:06:07,100
In its last full fiscal year, about
seventy-eight percent of its sales came

124
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:09,580
from what it calls recurrent
consumer spending.

125
00:06:09,580 --> 00:06:13,580
Money players spend after they've bought
a game: in-game currency, extra

126
00:06:13,580 --> 00:06:15,025
content, subscriptions.

127
00:06:15,025 --> 00:06:18,705
In other words, most of the time, the
first purchase isn't where most of

128
00:06:18,705 --> 00:06:19,705
the money is.

129
00:06:19,665 --> 00:06:20,865
Remember that number.

130
00:06:20,865 --> 00:06:23,105
We'll come back to it.

131
00:06:24,060 --> 00:06:26,220
Part 5 The British Taxpayer's Share

132
00:06:26,220 --> 00:06:29,180
Now for the part that
surprises most people.

133
00:06:29,180 --> 00:06:33,277
The Grand Theft Auto games are set in
fictional versions of American cities.

134
00:06:33,277 --> 00:06:37,437
But the series' lead studio, Rockstar
North, is in Edinburgh, Scotland.

135
00:06:37,437 --> 00:06:40,652
And the United Kingdom has a tax
relief for video games.

136
00:06:40,812 --> 00:06:44,812
If a game passes a test showing it's
culturally British, the studio can

137
00:06:44,812 --> 00:06:47,852
claim back part of what it
spends making it.

138
00:06:47,852 --> 00:06:51,136
Grand Theft Auto Five passed
that test in 2015.

139
00:06:51,136 --> 00:06:53,696
The test isn't only about
where a game is set.

140
00:06:53,696 --> 00:06:57,056
Points can also come from where the
work is done, and who does it.

141
00:06:57,056 --> 00:07:01,169
A game about a fictional Los Angeles, made
largely in Edinburgh, can qualify.

142
00:07:01,169 --> 00:07:04,609
In 2024, Britain replaced the old
relief with a new credit.

143
00:07:04,609 --> 00:07:08,369
Studios can now claim back thirty-four
percent of qualifying spending, before

144
00:07:08,369 --> 00:07:09,369
tax.

145
00:07:09,046 --> 00:07:12,966
According to an investigation by the
Scottish newspaper The Scotsman, Rockstar

146
00:07:12,966 --> 00:07:16,726
claimed about three hundred sixty million
pounds through that relief between

147
00:07:16,726 --> 00:07:18,406
2015 and 2023.

148
00:07:18,646 --> 00:07:21,686
About thirty-five percent of all the
money paid out under the scheme.

149
00:07:21,686 --> 00:07:24,966
A later report said the company received
more than seventy million pounds

150
00:07:24,966 --> 00:07:27,926
in a single year, 2024 to 2025.

151
00:07:28,484 --> 00:07:31,542
A research group called
TaxWatch UK went further.

152
00:07:31,542 --> 00:07:36,182
In reports from 2019 and 2020, it said
that Rockstar's UK companies had

153
00:07:36,182 --> 00:07:40,022
paid no UK corporation tax over the
previous ten years, even though their

154
00:07:40,022 --> 00:07:42,462
American parent company was
making large profits.

155
00:07:42,622 --> 00:07:45,582
And it said the big claims were likely
linked to the production of the next

156
00:07:45,582 --> 00:07:47,502
Grand Theft Auto.

157
00:07:47,742 --> 00:07:49,022
There's another side to this.

158
00:07:49,022 --> 00:07:53,511
The relief is legal, and it's designed to
do exactly this: attract big game

159
00:07:53,511 --> 00:07:54,791
productions to Britain.

160
00:07:54,791 --> 00:07:58,151
In response to the criticism, the company's
side has said the program helped

161
00:07:58,151 --> 00:08:02,558
it create well over a thousand long-term,
highly skilled jobs in the UK.

162
00:08:02,718 --> 00:08:06,318
And the industry's trade body says every
pound invested through the relief

163
00:08:06,318 --> 00:08:09,678
brings back about four pounds in
value to the economy.

164
00:08:10,078 --> 00:08:13,438
So when you buy Grand Theft Auto Six,
part of the cost of making it was

165
00:08:13,438 --> 00:08:14,438
already covered.

166
00:08:14,354 --> 00:08:16,034
By British taxpayers.

167
00:08:17,715 --> 00:08:19,795
Part 6 So, Who Gets Rich?

168
00:08:19,795 --> 00:08:22,675
So let's put the eighty
dollars back together.

169
00:08:22,755 --> 00:08:27,175
If you bought it digitally, about twenty-four
dollars went to Sony or Microsoft.

170
00:08:27,335 --> 00:08:31,095
If you bought a box, a store and the
console maker split around a third

171
00:08:31,095 --> 00:08:32,095
or more of it.

172
00:08:32,055 --> 00:08:34,375
Sales tax went to the government on top.

173
00:08:34,375 --> 00:08:38,587
The rest went to Take-Two, to pay for
years of development and marketing.

174
00:08:38,827 --> 00:08:42,507
Development that was partly paid
for by a British tax relief.

175
00:08:42,747 --> 00:08:43,947
But here's the thing.

176
00:08:43,947 --> 00:08:47,781
The biggest money in Grand Theft Auto
has never been the first sale.

177
00:08:47,781 --> 00:08:51,701
Grand Theft Auto Five kept earning for
thirteen years, through its online

178
00:08:51,701 --> 00:08:55,061
mode and the in-game money players
bought with real money.

179
00:08:55,061 --> 00:08:57,527
That's the seventy-eight
percent we saw earlier.

180
00:08:57,527 --> 00:09:01,047
With the new game, Take-Two is already
offering a free month of its paid

181
00:09:01,047 --> 00:09:03,207
subscription to people who pre-ordered.

182
00:09:03,687 --> 00:09:06,038
The eighty dollars is just the
ticket at the door.

183
00:09:06,038 --> 00:09:10,438
The real business is everything
people spend after they walk in.

184
00:09:10,838 --> 00:09:12,988
Next time, the story we promised.

185
00:09:13,148 --> 00:09:16,748
One of the oldest banks in the world,
Credit Suisse, survived for one hundred

186
00:09:16,748 --> 00:09:18,188
and sixty-seven years.

187
00:09:18,188 --> 00:09:20,127
Then it died in a single weekend.

188
